---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import ExecutionTrace from "../components/visual/ExecutionTrace.astro";
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";

const festival = (await getCollection("work")).find(
  (entry) => entry.data.name === "Festival",
);

if (!festival) {
  throw new Error("Festival work entry is missing");
}
---

<Base
  title="Work — Obedience Corp"
  description="Explore Camp and Fest, the Obey agent runtime, Festival App, Festival Activity, and our engineering engagements."
  current="/work"
>
  <section class="inner-hero inner-hero-split work-hero">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">SELECTED WORK</p>
      <h1>Work you can inspect, run, and judge for yourself.</h1>
      <p>
        Obedience Corp builds in the open where it can, and under NDA where it
        can’t. Camp and Fest organize our agent work, Obey manages execution,
        and Festival App brings the workspace to the desktop. Explore the
        available tools and the upcoming desktop app below.
      </p>
    </div>
    <ExecutionTrace title="PUBLIC ARTIFACT / FESTIVAL" compact />
  </section>

  <section class="work-case" id="festival">
    <header class="work-case-head">
      <div>
        <p class="section-label">01 / OPEN SOURCE</p>
        <h2>{festival.data.name}</h2>
      </div>
      <p>{festival.data.license?.toUpperCase()} · AVAILABLE NOW</p>
    </header>

    <div class="work-case-grid">
      <div class="work-case-copy">
        <p class="work-case-lede">
          Keep the way you work across agents and projects.
        </p>
        <p>
          A Festival camp workspace keeps plans, research, scripts, and workflow
          templates beside separately linked project repositories. You set the
          goal and review the plan. Your agent follows the steps with <code
            >fest next</code
          >, records results, and returns at the review points you set. A fresh
          session reads the saved state and instructions before continuing.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Camp</strong> keeps projects, worktrees, research, and decisions
          together across agent sessions. Link your existing repositories into a camp;
          each keeps its own Git history.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Fest</strong> turns goals into phases and tasks, guides agents to
          the next step, and tracks progress through review gates. Camp and Fest work
          with shell-capable agents such as Claude Code and Codex; Obey adds background
          execution when you want it.
        </p>
        <p>
          The public CodeSignal Practice Simulator shows the result of three
          completed festivals: a Python engine, browser IDE, and practice
          library. Its repository includes the plans, review findings, and
          verification records. One review found that repeated edits could hide
          older saved versions despite passing browser tests. The record shows
          the failing regression tests, the correction, and verification that a
          user could restore an older version.
        </p>
        <div class="inline-links">
          <a href="https://fest.build/stories/codesignal-practice-simulator"
            >Inspect the completed build ↗</a
          >
          <a href={festival.data.url}>Product site ↗</a>
          <a href="https://docs.fest.build">Documentation ↗</a>
          <a href="https://github.com/Obedience-Corp/festival">Source ↗</a>
          <a href="https://github.com/Obedience-Corp/camp">Camp source ↗</a>
          <a href="https://github.com/Obedience-Corp/fest">Fest source ↗</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <dl class="work-facts">
        <div>
          <dt>STATUS</dt>
          <dd>Public</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>LICENSE</dt>
          <dd>{festival.data.license}</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>SURFACE</dt>
          <dd>Command line</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>FOCUS</dt>
          <dd>Context, planning, execution, verification</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="work-case" id="obey">
    <header class="work-case-head">
      <div>
        <p class="section-label">02 / AGENT RUNTIME</p>
        <h2>Obey</h2>
      </div>
      <p>PROPRIETARY · AVAILABLE NOW</p>
    </header>

    <div class="work-case-grid">
      <div class="work-case-copy">
        <p class="work-case-lede">
          Follow your agents. Let Obey carry the execution.
        </p>
        <p>
          Obey is a local runtime that tracks agent sessions across your camps.
          It can observe supported sessions you start yourself, or launch and
          supervise agents for you. The live session view helps you see the work
          that needs your attention.
        </p>
        <p>
          Camp holds the workspace and Fest holds the plan. Obey reads that
          context to drive festival execution. Its companion <code>ob</code>{
            " "
          }
          CLI also handles developer tasks such as planning and reviewing changes.
        </p>
        <p>
          Start with Camp and Fest and the agent you already use. Add Obey when
          you want daemon-managed execution. Obey binaries are publicly
          available; its source is private.
        </p>
        <div class="inline-links">
          <a href="https://fest.build/#obey">Install and configure Obey ↗</a>
          <a href="https://fest.build/releases/obey/">Obey releases ↗</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <dl class="work-facts">
        <div><dt>STATUS</dt><dd>Available now</dd></div>
        <div><dt>LICENSE</dt><dd>Proprietary</dd></div>
        <div><dt>SURFACE</dt><dd>Local daemon and CLI</dd></div>
        <div><dt>FOCUS</dt><dd>Agent sessions and execution</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="work-case" id="festival-app">
    <header class="work-case-head">
      <div>
        <p class="section-label">03 / DESKTOP APP</p>
        <h2>Festival App</h2>
      </div>
      <p>PUBLIC RELEASE COMING SOON</p>
    </header>

    <div class="work-case-grid">
      <div class="work-case-copy">
        <p class="work-case-lede">
          A desktop home for your camps, agents, and festivals.
        </p>
        <p>
          Festival App brings camp browsing, saved context, agent sessions, and
          festival progress into one desktop workspace. It connects to Obey for
          agent sessions and uses Camp and Fest for the work kept on disk.
        </p>
        <p>
          Festival App is preparing for its first public release. Camp, Fest,
          and Obey are available through the CLI today. The app’s public launch
          will be announced on fest.build.
        </p>
        <div class="inline-links">
          <a href="https://fest.build/#app">About the upcoming app ↗</a>
          <a href="https://fest.build/#start">Use the CLI today ↗</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <dl class="work-facts">
        <div><dt>STATUS</dt><dd>Public release pending</dd></div>
        <div><dt>SURFACE</dt><dd>Desktop app</dd></div>
        <div><dt>CONNECTS</dt><dd>Camp, Fest, and Obey</dd></div>
        <div><dt>FOCUS</dt><dd>Workspace and agent interaction</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="work-case" id="festival-activity">
    <header class="work-case-head">
      <div>
        <p class="section-label">04 / ACTIVITY TIMELINE</p>
        <h2>Festival Activity</h2>
      </div>
      <p>MACOS AND LINUX · PAID</p>
    </header>

    <div class="work-case-grid">
      <div class="work-case-copy">
        <p class="work-case-lede">
          A desktop timeline of the work in your camps, built on your machine.
        </p>
        <p>
          Festival Activity reads your git history and your Festival progress
          and shows you the shape of a month, a quarter, or a single afternoon,
          across every camp you have. It reads and nothing else: it does not
          edit your documents, run agents, or send anything anywhere.
        </p>
        <p>
          Festival Activity is the separate, paid timeline product. Camp and
          Fest stay free and open source.
        </p>
        <div class="inline-links">
          <a href="/festival-activity">Festival Activity →</a>
          <a href="/festival-activity/terms">Terms of sale</a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <dl class="work-facts">
        <div>
          <dt>STATUS</dt>
          <dd>Paid, one purchase</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>RUNS ON</dt>
          <dd>macOS and Linux</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>NEEDS</dt>
          <dd>Camp and Fest, installed free</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>SENDS</dt>
          <dd>Nothing about your work</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="work-case client-work">
    <header class="work-case-head">
      <div>
        <p class="section-label">05 / CLIENT WORK</p>
        <h2>Under NDA</h2>
      </div>
      <p>2025 · NAMES WITHHELD</p>
    </header>

    <div class="work-case-grid">
      <div class="work-case-copy">
        <p class="work-case-lede">
          Three startups launched products built with these systems.
        </p>
        <p>
          The engagements are covered by agreements that do not allow us to name
          the companies or show the code, so we describe the shape of the work
          instead. In each case the same system carried it: a defined scope,
          context kept in files the client owns, execution across many sessions,
          and verification before anything shipped.
        </p>
        <p>
          If a reference matters before you commit, ask on a call. What we can
          share, we share directly. Festival above is the part you can audit
          line by line without asking anyone’s permission.
        </p>
      </div>

      <dl class="work-facts">
        <div>
          <dt>ENGAGEMENTS</dt>
          <dd>3 startups, 2025</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>OUTCOME</dt>
          <dd>Products launched</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>DISCLOSURE</dt>
          <dd>Names and code under NDA</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>SAME SYSTEM</dt>
          <dd>Scope, context, execution, verification</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="work-proof">
    <p class="section-label">WHAT IT PROVES</p>
    <div>
      <h2>Not a concept site. A working system built under load.</h2>
      <p>
        Festival grew out of using AI agents every day across software,
        research, and company operations. The code, documentation, and ongoing
        field notes are public so the work can be evaluated on substance.
      </p>
      <a href="/blog/nine-months-of-festival"
        >Read the nine-month field report →</a
      >
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="work-cta">
    <p class="section-label">APPLIED ENGAGEMENTS</p>
    <h2>Need this kind of reliability inside your own AI workflow?</h2>
    <a class="action action-primary" href="/#engagements"
      >See how an engagement works →</a
    >
  </section>
</Base>
